A Frobenius manifold is a smooth (or complex) manifold $M$ equipped with the following data:
(i) a commutative, associative, unital product $\circ$ on each tangent space $T_pM$, depending smoothly on $p$;
(ii) a flat (pseudo-)Riemannian metric $g$ on $M$ such that the structure constants of $\circ$ in flat coordinates are derivatives of a single function (the potential $F$), i.e. there exist flat coordinates $(t^1,\dots,t^n)$ in which $c_{ijk}=\partial_i\partial_j\partial_k F$ and $\eta_{ij}\,c^i{}_{jk}\eta^{kl}=\partial_j\partial_k\partial_l F\cdot\eta\text{-raisings}$;
(iii) a unit vector field $e$ which is $g$-flat and satisfies $e\circ X=X$ for all $X$;
(iv) an Euler vector field $E$ giving $F$ the appropriate quasi-homogeneity, with $\mathcal{L}_E\,g=(2-d)g$ for some constant $d$.

Two torsion-free affine connections $\nabla$ and $\widetilde\nabla$ on $M$ are projectively equivalent if they share the same unparametrised geodesics; equivalently, $\widetilde\nabla_X Y=\nabla_X Y+\alpha(X)Y+\alpha(Y)X$ for some $1$-form $\alpha$. A geometric structure on $M$ is called projectively invariant if it depends only on the projective class of the Levi-Civita connection of $g$ rather than on $g$ itself.

Questions:
(a) Is the Frobenius structure $(M,\circ,e,E,g)$ projectively invariant, in the sense that the multiplication $\circ$, unit $e$, Euler field $E$, and the associated WDVV/flat-pencil data depend only on the projective class of the Levi-Civita connection of $g$?
(b) Can the flatness assumption on $g$ in the definition of a Frobenius manifold be relaxed to require only that $g$ have constant sectional curvature, while preserving a coherent theory analogous to the standard Frobenius manifold theory (in particular admitting a potential $F$, flat pencil of metrics, and Dubrovin connection)?

